## Persistent C++ objects

JSI modules can also expose persistent C++ objects. Put `@SupernotePluginExport` on
the class or struct and on each method that JavaScript should see. Ordinary
unmarked public methods remain C++-only.

```cpp
// Counter.hpp
#pragma once

// @SupernotePluginExport
class Counter {
public:
  Counter(double initial);
  // @SupernotePluginExport
  double value() const;
  // @SupernotePluginExport
  void increment(double amount);
  void rebuildInternalCache();

private:
  double value_;
};
```

Implement the class in an ordinary `.cpp` file. The generated factory exposes
it as `$MODULE_NAME_RAW.Counter.create(...)`:

```typescript
const counter = $MODULE_NAME_RAW.Counter.create(10);
counter.increment(5);
console.log(counter.value());
```

Each returned JavaScript object represents a persistent native C++ instance.
Only explicitly marked methods enter JavaScript. Native objects are JSI-only,
and their constructors and methods remain synchronous in this starter.

## Call it from your plugin

```typescript
import $MODULE_NAME_RAW from '$NPM_NAME_RAW';

const total = $MODULE_NAME_RAW.add(20, 22);
```

**JSI runtime requirement:** The generated library may compile but still be
blocked by PluginHost or SELinux. Verify the target environment before relying
on it. Do not use JSI for blocking or long-running work.

```bash
supernote-module validate $NPM_NAME_RAW --build
```

Add `--verbose` if validation fails.

## Before Update or Remove

Update replaces this README and generated files, but preserves the implementation
folder above. Remove deletes the complete module. Commit first.

